  "prediction": "The report examines the differences in measuring the size of the federal workforce between the Office of Personnel Management (OPM) and the Office of Management and Budget (OMB). OPM uses on-board personnel data, which includes full-time and part-time employees, while OMB uses Full-Time Equivalent (FTE) data, which calculates the number of full-time employees required to perform the same work.\n\nThe report highlights the differences between FTE and on-board personnel data through examples. One FTE is equivalent to one person working full-time, while on-board personnel data includes part-time employees. Table 1 shows that there is a significant difference between the two methods, with OMB reporting a smaller federal workforce than OPM.\n\nOPM collects and maintains data on the federal workforce through its Employment and Holidays Reporting System (EHRS) and the Federal Employee Data System (FEDS). OPM's data is publicly available through its FedScope database, which provides information on employment trends, diversity, and demographics.\n\nOMB, on the other hand, calculates FTE data for budgeting purposes and is available in the President's Budget. The report provides examples of where to find federal employment data in the President's Budget, including the Analytical Perspectives, Appendix, and Historical Tables volumes.\n\nThe report concludes by emphasizing the importance of understanding the differences between FTE and on-board personnel data when using federal workforce statistics from OPM and OMB. Users should be aware of these differences to ensure accurate and consistent data analysis.",
  "scored_prediction": "The report examines the differences in measuring the size of the federal workforce between the Office of Personnel Management (OPM) and the Office of Management and Budget (OMB). OPM uses on-board personnel data, which includes full-time and part-time employees, while OMB uses Full-Time Equivalent (FTE) data, which calculates the number of full-time employees required to perform the same work.\n\nThe report highlights the differences between FTE and on-board personnel data through examples. One FTE is equivalent to one person working full-time, while on-board personnel data includes part-time employees. Table 1 shows that there is a significant difference between the two methods, with OMB reporting a smaller federal workforce than OPM.\n\nOPM collects and maintains data on the federal workforce through its Employment and Holidays Reporting System (EHRS) and the Federal Employee Data System (FEDS). OPM's data is publicly available through its FedScope database, which provides information on employment trends, diversity, and demographics.\n\nOMB, on the other hand, calculates FTE data for budgeting purposes and is available in the President's Budget. The report provides examples of where to find federal employment data in the President's Budget, including the Analytical Perspectives, Appendix, and Historical Tables volumes.\n\nThe report concludes by emphasizing the importance of understanding the differences between FTE and on-board personnel data when using federal workforce statistics from OPM and OMB. Users should be aware of these differences to ensure accurate and consistent data analysis.",
  "score": 23.834196441139365,
  "answers": [
    "This report describes online tools, reports, and data compilations created by the Office of Management and Budget (OMB) and the Office of Personnel Management (OPM) that contain statistics about federal employees and the federal workforce. The report also describes key characteristics of each resource and briefly discusses selected methodological differences, with the intention of facilitating the selection of appropriate data for specific purposes. This report is not intended to be a definitive list of all information on the federal workforce. It describes significant and recurring products that contain specific data often requested by Members or congressional staff."
  ],
